 "licence_note": null,
 "history": "Slops were the seaman's own breeches: loose, straight-cut, of canvas or old sailcloth, pleated into a waistband and ending just below the knee, open there, worn by no other trade; wider 'petticoat breeches' were older and rarer [D1][D2]. Joseph Haycock's slop shop in 1699 sold them among its breeches [D2]. The Drift crews tar the hems and patch the knees with retired sails.",
